  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 222 AM EDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nCurrent-Tuesday...Surface high pressure remains aligned across the \neastern CONUS as weak low pressure develops along an old frontal \nboundary between the Bahamas and FL Straits. This will keep a\ntight pressure gradient as persistent, gusty NERLY winds continue \nacross ECFL. Highest winds become sustained 20-25 mph with \nfrequent gusts to 30-35 mph and possibly 40 mph along the coast \ntoday. Will continue a Wind Advisory along the coast through\n00Z/8PM. Elsewhere winds across ECFL generally 15-20 mph with \nfrequent gusts to 25-30 mph. Winds will decrease into the evening \nto around 10 mph with some higher gusts over the interior, but \nstill breezy (15-20 mph & gusty) at the coast. Scattered (30-50%) \nonshore-moving squalls will continue today/tonight rapidly moving\nfrom northeast to southwest and may produce isolated wind gusts \nof 40-50 mph. Highest shower chances today along the coast. Brief\ndownpours will occur and minor flooding remains in play for \nlocations (coast) that receive multiple rounds, esp over the past \nfew days. \n\nElevated/gusty onshore winds will persist into Tue, but highest\nrain chances shunted further south across the Treasure Coast\n(40-70%), tapering back to 10-30% north/west.\n\nAfternoon max temps remain seasonal, near 80F to L80s each day. Min \novernight temps in the M-U60s well into the interior and near 70 to \nL70s toward the coast where winds remain elevated. \n\nPlenty of dangerous coastal/marine hazards as numerous, strong, life-\nthreatening rip currents and rough surf remain present at all \nlocal beaches. A High Surf Advisory remains in effect for large \nbreaking waves 7-11 ft in the surf zone. Entering the water is \nnot advised! Pounding surf and persistent long period onshore \nswells will continue Moderate to Major beach erosion at times of \nhigh tide. This significant coastal event coincides with a period \nof astronomical high tides, and Minor to Moderate flooding is \nalso forecast. During the morning high tide cycles saltwater \nflooding will impact low-lying roads, lots, docks, and yards near \nthe coast and intracoastal waterways. A Coastal Flood Warning \nremains in effect.\n\nWed-Sun...High pressure across the eastern U.S. becomes\nreinforced, but does show a weakening trend late in the period \nas it is pushed into the western Altc with approach of a \ndeveloping low pressure system. Breezy/gusty conditions continue \ninto late work-week as the pgrad remains rather tight. Poor to \nhazardous coastal/marine conditions continue alongside the period \nof high astronomical tide cycles. Highest PoPs occur Wed/Thu (esp\nCape southward) during this period as another surge of moisture \nis poised to move across the region. Near to slightly above normal\ntemperatures are forecast with highs in the L-M80s. Lows continue \nabove climo well into the 60s to L70s at least thru Sat morning.",
